3:T7f0,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"Blog","@id":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/blog#blog","url":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/blog","name":"Knowledge Hub","description":"Practical, opinionated writing on content architecture, headless CMS, and shipping real products with Sanity and Next.js.\n\nEvery post comes from decisions already baked into this starter: how to model content that scales, query it cleanly, and turn structure into pages search engines and AI assistants can actually read.","inLanguage":"en","isPartOf":{"@id":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/#website"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/#organization"},"blogPost":[{"@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/blog/serve-content-to-ai-agents-llms-txt-markdown-sanity","url":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/blog/serve-content-to-ai-agents-llms-txt-markdown-sanity","headline":"Your Site Is Invisible to AI Agents (and Your CMS Can Fix It)","description":"AI agents read your site as token-heavy HTML and cite it badly. Two CMS features fix it: an editor-owned llms.txt generated by AI, and per-page Markdown served to agents on the same URL.","datePublished":"2026-06-28"},{"@type":"BlogPosting","@id":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/blog/stale-sanity-content-nextjs-caching-revalidation","url":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/blog/stale-sanity-content-nextjs-caching-revalidation","headline":"Stale Sanity Content in Next.js and Astro: The Two Caches and How to Fix Them","description":"Sanity content not updating in Next.js or Astro, or only showing after a redeploy? Two caches cause it: the Sanity CDN and the framework's own cache. How each one works and how to fix stale content.","datePublished":"2026-06-27"}]},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://www.contentarchitecture.dev/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Knowledge Hub","item":"https:/